      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To compute the value of the given summation \\[\\lfloor \\sqrt{1} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{2} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{3} \\rfloor + \\cdots + \\lfloor \\sqrt{16} \\rfloor,\\] we need to evaluate the floor function, denoted by $\\lfloor x \\rfloor$, which takes the floor of $x$. This means taking the largest integer that is less than or equal to $x$. Because the function $\\lfloor \\sqrt{x} \\rfloor$ changes value at each integer square root of $x$, we can group these square roots based on their values.\n\nThe summation can be broken down as follows:\n\\[\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{1} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{2} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{3} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{4} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{5} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{6} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{7} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{8} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{9} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{10} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{11} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{12} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{13} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{14} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{15} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{16} \\rfloor.\n\\]\n\nThe values of the floor function $\\lfloor \\sqrt{x} \\rfloor$ for each interval from $1$ to $16$ are:\n- For $1 \\leq x < 2$, $\\lfloor \\sqrt{x} \\rfloor = 1$.\n- For $2 \\leq x < 4$, $\\lfloor \\sqrt{x} \\rfloor = 2$.\n- For $4 \\leq x < 9$, $\\lfloor \\sqrt{x} \\rfloor = 3$.\n- For $9 \\leq x < 16$, $\\lfloor \\sqrt{x} \\rfloor = 4$.\n\nWe can now compute each part of the summation accordingly:\n\\[\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{1} \\rfloor = 1,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{2} \\rfloor = 1,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{3} \\rfloor = 1,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{4} \\rfloor = 2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{5} \\rfloor = 2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{6} \\rfloor = 2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{7} \\rfloor = 2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{8} \\rfloor = 2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{9} \\rfloor = 3,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{10} \\rfloor = 3,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{11} \\rfloor = 3,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{12} \\rfloor = 3,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{13} \\rfloor = 3,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{14} \\rfloor = 3,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{15} \\rfloor = 3,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{16} \\rfloor = 4.\n\\]\n\nSumming up all these values:\n\\[\n1 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 4 = 36.\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{36}\\).\n\nNow we'll confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Defining the floor function for the square root\r\nimport math\r\n\r\n# Calculate the sum of floor(sqrt(x)) for x from 1 to 16\r\ntotal_sum = sum(math.floor(math.sqrt(x)) for x in range(1, 17))\r\nprint(total_sum)\n```",
